<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pemmican War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1812">1812</a>–<a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pemmican War was a conflict between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-company">North West Company</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-colony">Red River Colony</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a>. It began when the colony's governor banned the export of pemmican, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> and fur traders' staple food, in 1814. The violence peaked at Seven Oaks in 1816, when twenty-one colonists were killed, and ended with the merger of the two companies in 1821.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pemmican-war.jpg" alt="Pemmican War" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles William Jefferys · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Fight_at_Seven_Oaks.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-douglas-5th-earl-of-selkirk">Thomas Douglas, 5th Earl of Selkirk</a>, wished to resettle Scots in the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. From 1808 he bought shares of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>, whose stock had fallen under competition from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-company">North West Company</a>, and gained the land for a colony on the Red River. Settlers sailed in July 1811 under the appointed governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miles-macdonell">Miles Macdonell</a>.</p><p>The colony lay across the supply lines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-company">North West Company</a>, which, unlike its rival, fed its canoe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> on pemmican, dried buffalo meat pounded and mixed with fat. It bought most of this from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a>, who were largely tied to the company and opposed both the colony and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>. On 8 January 1814, with provisions scarce, Macdonell forbade anyone to take provisions out of the territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1814 the colonists blockaded the rivers and seized a cache of 96 bags of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-company">North West Company</a>'s pemmican, and in October Macdonell ordered its posts abandoned, invoking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>'s charter. In 1815 the partner Duncan Cameron worked to drive the settlers out by threats and offers of passage to Upper Canada. A new governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-semple">Robert Semple</a>, arrived that November with about 160 settlers and rebuilt the colony, and in March 1816 Colin Robertson seized Fort Gibraltar, which Semple had dismantled and burned.</p><p>At Seven Oaks, Semple led about 20 men out from Fort Douglas to meet a party of mounted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> and Canadians that grew to about 60. Shots followed an argument with the clerk François-Firmin Boucher, and Semple's men were cut down. Selkirk, coming with soldiers, seized Fort William, and in January 1817 Fort Douglas was retaken without a shot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Commissioner William Coltman brought a proclamation from the Prince Regent that produced a ceasefire, heard witnesses at Red River and in 1818 reported that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> had been the first aggressor and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-company">North West Company</a> had gone far beyond self-defence. Charges ran to 38 murders, 60 cases of grand larceny and 13 of arson, but only a handful were tried, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. Every trial ended in acquittal but one: Charles De Reinhard was sentenced to death for the murder of Owen Keveney, and the sentence was never carried out.</p><p>Clashes continued at the Grand Rapids portage in 1819 and 1820. Falling profits and pressure from the British government brought the two companies together in 1821, and many former opponents then worked side by side.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pemmican War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Pemmican War</b>, men of the Hudson's Bay Company and the North West Company fought a violent confrontation near modern-day Winnipeg, also called the Seven Oaks Massacre.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-19">19 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
